":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Investment holdings halved to \u00a38.5m while net borrowing increased 47%, reducing liquidity buffers","lead":"Reduced liquidity limits flexibility to handle contingencies or market disruptions, increasing dependency on continuous access to capital markets for refinancing.","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"Audit Committee Treasury Outturn Report 2025 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86463/86463.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Economic outlook shifted toward higher inflation and rates, contradicting the council's rate-fall assumption","lead":"If refinancing occurs at current elevated rates rather than anticipated lower rates, revenue costs will rise significantly, eroding the \u00a3200k annual saving from the earlier loan refinancing and creating budget pressure.","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"Audit Committee Treasury Outturn Report 2025 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86463/86463.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Capital Financing Requirement grew 20% to \u00a399.9m but borrowing remained flat, funded from diminishing reserves","lead":"If capital spending continues and rates do not fall as assumed, reserves could deplete faster than planned, constraining financial flexibility and forcing either higher borrowing costs or capital programme cuts.","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"Audit Committee Treasury Outturn Report 2025 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86463/86463.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Audit Committee Treasury Outturn Report 2025 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86463/86463.pdf","headline":"Investment holdings halved to \u00a38.5m while net borrowing increased 47%, reducing liquidity buffers","detail":"Treasury investments fell from \u00a317.0m to \u00a38.5m (50% reduction), while net borrowing rose from \u00a321.5m to \u00a331.5m. The remaining investments are primarily instant-access bank deposits (\u00a35.8m) and money market funds (\u00a32.7m). This reduction in liquid holdings constrains the council's ability to manage unexpected capital needs or cash flow volatility.","category":"reserves","matters":"Reduced liquidity limits flexibility to handle contingencies or market disruptions, increasing dependency on continuous access to capital markets for refinancing.","figures":"Investments: \u00a317,015k (2025) to \u00a38,530k (2026); net borrowing: \u00a321,485k to \u00a331,470k (47% increase)","quote":"The \u00a38,530,000 investments at the year-end comprised of; \u00a32,710,000 Money Market Funds, and \u00a35,820,000 in instant access bank accounts.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Audit Committee Treasury Outturn Report 2025 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86463/86463.pdf","headline":"Economic outlook shifted toward higher inflation and rates, contradicting the council's rate-fall assumption","detail":"The council's treasury strategy assumes rates will fall to permit cheaper refinancing. However, the economic outlook has deteriorated materially. The document forecasts inflation exceeding 4% and notes that rate hikes are now priced into markets. 1-year PWLB rates at year-end (5.04%) are 153bp higher than the 3.51% at which the council recently borrowed.","category":"borrowing","matters":"If refinancing occurs at current elevated rates rather than anticipated lower rates, revenue costs will rise significantly, eroding the \u00a3200k annual saving from the earlier loan refinancing and creating budget pressure.","figures":"Bank Rate cuts: three 0.25% cuts in 2025/26, from 4.5% to 3.75%; PWLB 1-year rate 5.04%; inflation forecast 4%+ in 2026/27","quote":"Heading into 2026/27, UK inflation is likely to increase to over 4% in the coming months as oil prices, for example, remain close to $100 per barrel, over 50% higher than before the Middle East conflict started.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Audit Committee Treasury Outturn Report 2025 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86463/86463.pdf","headline":"Capital Financing Requirement grew 20% to \u00a399.9m but borrowing remained flat, funded from diminishing reserves","detail":"CFR increased from \u00a383.4m to \u00a399.9m, driven by \u00a318.6m unfinanced capital expenditure, but gross borrowing stayed at \u00a340m. The council is funding two-thirds of its borrowing need from cash reserves rather than market borrowing. However, investment balances halved from \u00a317m to \u00a38.5m, reducing the buffer available for this strategy.","category":"capital","matters":"If capital spending continues and rates do not fall as assumed, reserves could deplete faster than planned, constraining financial flexibility and forcing either higher borrowing costs or capital programme cuts.","figures":"CFR increase \u00a383,431k to \u00a399,948k (20% growth); under-borrowing \u00a359,948k; investments \u00a317,015k to \u00a38,530k (50% decline)","quote":"During 2025/26, the Authority maintained an under-borrowed position. This meant that the capital borrowing need, (the Capital Financing Requirement), was not fully funded with loan debt as cash supporting the Authority's reserves, balances and cash flow was used as an interim measure.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Audit Committee Treasury Outturn Report 2025 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86463/86463.pdf","headline":"All \u00a340m of borrowing matures within 12 months, requiring refinancing in 2026/27","detail":"The council has three short-term loans totaling \u00a340m due to mature during 2026/2027. The largest is a \u00a328m PWLB loan borrowed at 3.51% for a 12-month term. This concentrates the entire debt portfolio into a single refinancing event during a period when 1-year PWLB rates stand at 5.04%, creating material interest rate risk.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Simultaneous refinancing of \u00a340m exposes the council to rate risk and market access risk; if rates remain elevated or credit conditions tighten, refinancing costs could increase sharply.","figures":"\u00a340m total short-term borrowing; \u00a328m PWLB at 3.51% (12-month term); current 1-year PWLB rate 5.04%; rate spread 153bp","quote":"As at the 31 March 2026 the council had three short-term loans with a collective value of \u00a340,000,000 due to mature during 2026/2027.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Cabinet - 2026-2027 Budget Monitoring Report Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86348/86348.pdf","headline":"West Norfolk Racket and Community Sports Hub project scope expanded significantly with tight delivery timescale","detail":"The project has been upgraded from tier 3 to tier 1 status with budget increasing from \u00a3866,730 to \u00a32,200,000. The scope now includes three covered padel courts, weatherproofed tennis courts and a multi-use games area, part-funded by a \u00a31,000,000 grant. The council is targeting completion within the current financial year.","category":"capital","matters":"A 154% budget increase on a project with tight timescales and external funding dependency creates delivery risk; budget overruns or delays could require reserve drawdown","figures":"Budget increased from \u00a3866,730 to \u00a32,200,000; \u00a31,000,000 grant funded","quote":"A report approved at Council on 29 July has seen the scope of this project increased to three covered padel courts, weatherproofed tennis courts and a multi-use games area. The budget requirement for the works is \u00a32,200,000, part funded with a grant of \u00a31,000,000.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-14"},"name":"King's Lynn & West Norfolk","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":false,"headline":"Investment holdings halved to \u00a38.5m while net borrowing increased 47%, reducing liquidity buffers","lead":"Reduced liquidity limits flexibility to handle contingencies or market disruptions, increasing dependency on continuous access to capital markets for refinancing.","also":["11 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-10-13","n_findings":12,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Audit Committee Treasury Outturn Report 2025 2026 Final","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.west-norfolk.gov.uk/documents/s86463/86463.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":83,"news_drafts_total":3}}